{"subjectId":"java","id":"overriding-vs-overloading","title":"Method Overriding vs Overloading","group":"Inheritance & Polymorphism","order":26,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Overloading = same name, different parameters, chosen at compile time. Overriding = same signature in a subclass, chosen at runtime.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-table\"></i>Side by side</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Overloading</th><th>Overriding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Where</td><td>Same class (or inherited)</td><td>Subclass redefines a superclass/interface method</td></tr><tr><td>Signature</td><td>Same name, <strong>different parameter list</strong></td><td>Same name <strong>and</strong> same parameters</td></tr><tr><td>Return type</td><td>Anything</td><td>Same or a subtype (<em>covariant</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>Access</td><td>Anything</td><td>Same or <strong>wider</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Checked exceptions</td><td>Anything</td><td>Same, narrower or none — never new/broader</td></tr><tr><td>Resolved</td><td>Compile time (static binding)</td><td>Runtime (dynamic dispatch)</td></tr><tr><td><code>static</code> / <code>private</code> / <code>final</code></td><td>Can be overloaded</td><td>Cannot be overridden</td></tr><tr><td>Polymorphism kind</td><td>Compile-time</td><td>Runtime</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-copy\"></i>Overloading</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static String show(int x)          { return \"int \" + x; }\n    static String show(long x)         { return \"long \" + x; }\n    static String show(double x)       { return \"double \" + x; }\n    static String show(Integer x)      { return \"Integer \" + x; }\n    static String show(Object x)       { return \"Object \" + x; }\n    static String show(int... xs)      { return \"varargs \" + xs.length; }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        byte b = 5;\n        System.out.println(show(7));        // exact match\n        System.out.println(show(b));        // widening byte → int beats boxing\n        System.out.println(show(7L));\n        System.out.println(show(7.0f));     // float → double\n        System.out.println(show(\"hi\"));     // String → Object\n        System.out.println(show());         // only varargs fits\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>int 7\nint 5\nlong 7\ndouble 7.0\nObject hi\nvarargs 0</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Overload resolution picks the most specific method in three phases: (1) exact/widening, (2) boxing/unboxing, (3) varargs. The return type alone never distinguishes overloads.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-replace\"></i>Overriding with <code>@Override</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Shape s = new Circle(2);           // reference type Shape, object Circle\n        System.out.println(s.name());      // runtime: Circle.name()\n        Circle copy = new Circle(2).copy();   // covariant return: no cast needed\n        System.out.printf(\"%.2f%n\", copy.area());\n    }\n}\n\nclass Shape {\n    protected String name() { return \"shape\"; }\n    Shape copy() { return new Shape(); }\n    double area() { return 0; }\n}\n\nclass Circle extends Shape {\n    private final double r;\n    Circle(double r) { this.r = r; }\n\n    @Override public String name() { return \"circle\"; }        // protected → public: widening OK\n    @Override Circle copy() { return new Circle(r); }          // covariant return type\n    @Override double area() { return Math.PI * r * r; }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>circle\n12.57</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Always write <code>@Override</code>. Without it, a typo or wrong parameter type silently creates an <em>overload</em> instead — e.g. <code>public boolean equals(Point p)</code> does not override <code>equals(Object)</code>.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>class Point {\n    int x;\n    @Override\n    public boolean equals(Point other) { return other.x == x; }   // wrong parameter type\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>javac</figcaption><pre><code>Main.java:3: error: method does not override or implement a method from a supertype\n    @Override\n    ^</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-gavel\"></i>Rules the compiler enforces</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.io.IOException;\n\nclass Parent {\n    public void run() { }\n    void load() throws IOException { }\n}\n\nclass Child extends Parent {\n    @Override protected void run() { }   // narrowing public → protected\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>javac</figcaption><pre><code>Main.java:9: error: run() in Child cannot override run() in Parent\n    @Override protected void run() { }   // narrowing public → protected\n                             ^\n  attempting to assign weaker access privileges; was public</code></pre></figure>\n<ul><li>An override <strong>may</strong> throw fewer or narrower checked exceptions (<code>FileNotFoundException</code> instead of <code>IOException</code>), or none; unchecked exceptions are unrestricted.</li><li><code>final</code> methods can't be overridden; <code>private</code> methods aren't inherited, so a same-named subclass method is just a new method.</li><li>Call the parent version with <code>super.method()</code>.</li></ul>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-eye-off\"></i>Static methods are hidden, not overridden</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Parent p = new Child();\n        System.out.println(p.stat());      // static: chosen by reference type\n        System.out.println(p.inst());      // instance: chosen by object type\n        System.out.println(p.label);       // fields are hidden too\n        System.out.println(((Child) p).label);\n    }\n}\n\nclass Parent {\n    String label = \"parent-field\";\n    static String stat() { return \"Parent.stat\"; }\n    String inst() { return \"Parent.inst\"; }\n}\n\nclass Child extends Parent {\n    String label = \"child-field\";\n    static String stat() { return \"Child.stat\"; }\n    @Override String inst() { return \"Child.inst\"; }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Parent.stat\nChild.inst\nparent-field\nchild-field</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Can we overload main?</summary><div><p>Yes — any number of <code>main</code> overloads may exist, but the JVM only launches <code>public static void main(String[] args)</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Can we override a static or private method?</summary><div><p>No. A static method with the same signature <em>hides</em> the parent's (resolved by reference type at compile time). A private method isn't visible to the subclass, so a same-named method is a brand-new method.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Can two overloads differ only by return type?</summary><div><p>No. The call <code>f(1)</code> wouldn't tell the compiler which one you want, so it's a compile error (<code>method f(int) is already defined</code>).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is a covariant return type?</summary><div><p>An overriding method may return a <em>subtype</em> of the parent method's return type — e.g. <code>Circle copy()</code> overriding <code>Shape copy()</code> — so callers need no cast. <code>clone()</code> overrides commonly use it.</p></div></details></section>"}